course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 32
title: Olympiad: endings and polite forms ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Ukrainian words change their ends to tell you things English says with whole extra words, and a sharp puzzler can read a whole scene from one ending.
explain: Endings tell you who is talking: a boy says ya vyhRAv, a girl ya vyhRAla; a boy is ya hoLOdnyi, a girl ya hoLOdna — so a note saying «ya vpala» was written by a girl.
explain: Endings call people: MAma → MAmo!, TAto → TAtu!, BAbusya → BAbusyu!, TItka → TItko! — find the pattern and you can shout any name the right way.
explain: Endings show respect: sidai to a kid but sidaite to an adult, vybaCHAI to a friend but vybaCHte to a stranger, i tobI to a friend but i vam to a granny.
explain: Picture Sashko at his granny's door: «zdorOv, BAbusyu!» would make her eyebrows jump, but «DObryi DEN, BAbusyu! yak vy?» gets him a hug and a pocketful of tsukErky.
fact: The calling form, the vocative, changes the end of a name: MAma → MAmo!, TAto → TAtu!, BAbusya → BAbusyu!, TItka → TItko!
fact: Many names ending in -a are called with -o: Oksana → Oksano!, Olena → Oleno!; Sashko, like TAto, is called with -u: Sashku!
fact: diDUS, grandad, is called diDUsyu!, just as BAbusya is called BAbusyu!
fact: In the past, a boy's word ends in -v and a girl's in -la: ya vyhRAv / ya vyhRAla, ya vpAv / ya vpala, ya ne zrobyv / ya ne zrobyla.
fact: Describing words change too: a boy is hoLOdnyi, radyi, PERshyi; a girl is hoLOdna, rada, PERsha.
fact: The comeback «takes one to know one» is sam takYi! from a boy and sama taka! from a girl.
fact: dva becomes dvi before some words such as DIvchyny: dvi DIvchyny, two girls.
fact: ty is «you» to one friend or child; vy is the polite «you» to one grown-up, and also «you all» to a group, even a group of kids.
fact: Many polite or group forms end in -te: sidai → sidaite (sit down), vybaCHAI → vybaCHte (sorry), ZAkhody → zaKHOdte (come in).
fact: With ty you answer i tobI (you too), with vy you answer i vam: «SMAchnoho!» — «DYAkuyu, i vam!»
fact: yak teBE zvut? asks a kid's name; yak vas zvut? is the polite form.
fact: pryVIT and zdorOv are hellos for friends, and zdorOv is never for a granny or a teacher; DObryi DEN is the safe hello to any grown-up.
fact: AHA and nE-a are casual yeah and nope between kids; tak and ni are safe to anyone.
exercise: MAma → MAmo!, TItka → TItko!. How do you call a girl named Oksana? | Oksano! | Oksanu! | Oksanyu! | Oksana!
exercise: TAto → TAtu!. How do you shout for Sashko across the yard? | Sashku! | Sashko! | Sashka! | Sashkyu!
exercise: BAbusya → BAbusyu!. How do you call diDUS, grandad? | diDUsyu! | diDUso! | diDUsa! | diDUsye!
exercise: Who wrote the note «ya vpala»? | A girl, from the -la ending | A boy, from the word ya | A teacher, about the class | Two boys, writing together
exercise: A boy wants to say «I didn't do it». Which is right? | ya ne zrobyv | ya ne zrobyla | ya ne zrobyly | ty ne zrobyv
exercise: A girl crosses the line first. What does she shout? | ya PERsha! | ya PERshyi! | ya PERshe! | ya ostANnya!
exercise: You splash juice on a stranger's coat. Which sorry fits? | vybaCHte | vybaCHAI | proBAch | DYAka
exercise: The friend's mum says «SMAchnoho!». What is the best answer? | DYAkuyu, i vam! | DYAkuyu, i tobI! | AHA, and you | zdorOv, TItko!
exercise: Which of these could only be said by a girl? | ya rada | ya PERshyi | sam takYi! | ya vyhRAv
exercise: Odd one out: pryVIT, zdorOv, AHA, DObryi DEN — which one is fine for a teacher? | DObryi DEN | pryVIT | zdorOv | AHA
exercise: Which ending can make a word polite to one grown-up? | -te, as in sidaite | -v, as in vpAv | -o, as in MAmo | -la, as in vyhRAla
exercise: The teacher says «sidaite!» to the whole class. Why -te, when the kids aren't grown-ups? | -te is also for a group: «you all» | Because she is very cross with them | Because sidai is rude to everybody | Because -te is only used on Mondays
exercise: «Sashko, ty proHRAv». Who is that said to? | One friend | A whole class of kids | A granny, politely | A group of grown-ups
exercise: Two girls and one boy all won a race. How many of them say ya vyhRAla? | Two | Three | Only one | None
exercise: ZAkhody! is come in! to a kid. What do you say to a granny at the door? | zaKHOdte! | zakhodYla! | ZAkhody, BAbusya! | zakhodYv!
exercise: Which greeting would make a granny's eyebrows jump? | zdorOv, BAbusyu! | DObryi DEN, BAbusyu! | DObryi DEN! yak vy? | DObroho RANku, BAbusyu!
test: Group chat: «ya vyhRAla!», «ya vpAv lol», «ya rada», «ya vtomYvsya». How many girls wrote? | Two | One | Three | Four
test: «I've told you a MILLION times — say vy to Granny!» shouts Mum. What does she really mean? | She has told you many times: use vy | She has counted to a million exactly | Granny is a million years old | She wants you to say ty to Granny
test: A kid asks the head teacher «yak teBE zvut?». What's wrong? | teBE is the ty form; to an adult it's yak vas zvut? | Nothing at all, it is perfect | He should shout «MAmo!» first | He should ask it in English
test: Sashko's sister texts «ya hoLOdnyi, literally dying». What's off, and how hungry is she? | The girl form is hoLOdna; she's just hungry | Nothing; she is really dying of hunger | She should write khoLOdnyi, which is cold | The boy form is right for everyone
test: A boy shouts «sama taka!» back at his mate. Fix it. | sam takYi! | sama taka! is right for him | sami taki! | sam taka!
test: «vy is only for grown-ups, you never ever say it to kids» says your mate. Check it. | Wrong: it is also «you all» to a group of kids | Right: vy is only ever said to grown-ups | Wrong: vy is a rude word, whoever you say it to | Right: vy really means «me», not «you»
test: You bump your best mate in the corridor. Which sorry fits best? | vybaCHAI | vybaCHte | do pobAchennya | DObryi DEN
test: «that went a bit wrong» says Sashko after shouting «zdorOv!» at the head teacher. How bad was it really? | Quite bad: zdorOv is never for teachers | No problem: zdorOv is the polite hello | A tiny slip: it means goodbye | Fine: zdorOv means good afternoon
test: A puzzle card: a boy says «sidaite», «vybaCHte» and «DYAkuyu, i vam». Who is he most likely talking to? | A grown-up, or a group of people | His little brother, alone | His best mate, alone | His cat
test: How do you say «two girls»? | dvi DIvchyny | dva DIvchyny | dvo DIvchyny | dvi DIvchyna
test: Odd one out by who could say it: ya PERsha, ya rada, ya vpala, ya hoLOdnyi. | ya hoLOdnyi: a boy's form | ya PERsha: it is about racing | ya rada: it is about the radio | ya vpala: it is about the past
test: «I nearly died when you said ty to Granny!» says Mum. What really happened? | Mum was shocked, not hurt | Mum was badly ill all day | Granny fainted on the spot | Mum laughed and agreed with you
exam: Determine the vocative of the name Oksana, by analogy with MAma → MAmo! | Oksano! | Oksanu! | Oksana! | Oksanyu!
exam: State the sex of the writer of «ya ne zrobyla», together with the evidence. | A girl, from the ending -la | A boy, from the ending -la | A boy, from the word ya | It cannot be determined from one sentence
exam: Select the expression of apology appropriate to an elderly stranger. | vybaCHte | vybaCHAI | DYAka | AHA
exam: Ascertain why sidaite may properly be addressed to a class of children. | The -te form serves both for the polite address to one adult and for several persons | The form is reserved for scolding | Children must be addressed as elders by law | The form denotes the past tense
